From: fydroid's mesa patch 021, rebased onto 26.1.8
Subject: [PATCH] zink: do not populate an attachment shadow while blitting

`begin_rendering()` populates a transient attachment and then calls itself.
The recursion terminates only if the second call finds the condition false --
and when the populate is itself driven from inside a u_blitter blit, it does
not, so the two keep calling each other until the rendering thread walks off
its stack. The result is a SIGSEGV with SEGV_ACCERR on the guard page,
preceded by

    u_blitter:559: Caught recursion. This is a driver bug.

which is the only part of it that names a cause. `!ctx->blitting` is the
termination condition that was missing.

Upstream issue 14269 (Firefox, zink, NVIDIA) is still open, and the guard is
unchanged in 26.1.3 and in 26.1.8 -- so this is carried, not waited for. If it
lands upstream in a different shape, drop this and take that instead.

Two independent things arm it, and only one of them is MSAA (dc, who found it
and fixed it on the fydroid side, and who read `zink_set_framebuffer_state` to
establish this):

  * `ctx->fb_state.cbufs[i].nr_samples` non-zero sets `transient_msrtss` --
    MSRTT emulation, which is WebGL with `antialias: true` and some canvas
    paths.
  * `framebuffer_surface_needs_mutable()` on a resource with an
    `immutable_handle` sets a transient attachment with `transient_msrtss`
    left false, which takes the first branch of the guard. That is format-view
    shadowing on an image that cannot be recreated mutable -- an imported or
    WSI-backed image rendered through a different format view, i.e. sRGB
    against linear on a dma-buf. That is ordinary browser compositing, and it
    is the one that fired for fydroid: WebView into gralloc-imported buffers,
    on first launch, every time.

So the path is entered readily; what makes the crash intermittent is that the
populate also has to land while a blit is in flight. That is content- and
timing-dependent, which is why it did not reproduce in the Notialis Firefox
runs and why those runs do not rule it out. Carried on that basis: the guard
is inert unless the recursion would actually be entered, so it costs nothing
when it is not needed, and the failure it prevents is a hard crash with no
useful signal unless you already know the u_blitter line is the tell.

--- a/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_context.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_context.c
@@ -3208,7 +3208,7 @@
    }
    attachment_shadow_mask &= ctx->transient_attachments;
    /* both msrtss emulation and format view shadowing use this path to populate the transient image */
-   if (((!ctx->transient_msrtss && ctx->rp_draw) || (ctx->transient_msrtss && !has_msrtss)) && attachment_shadow_mask && check_attachment_shadow) {
+   if (((!ctx->transient_msrtss && ctx->rp_draw) || (ctx->transient_msrtss && !has_msrtss)) && attachment_shadow_mask && check_attachment_shadow && !ctx->blitting) {
       zink_render_attachment_shadow(ctx, attachment_shadow_mask);
       return begin_rendering(ctx, false);
    }
